### 2026-08-15T12:13:53.095Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Good call — this deserves real analysis before another patch-and-move-on cycle.

**What actually happened:** `[REDACTED_PERSON]`'s mobile-drawer logic references DOM ids by string literal (`document.getElementById('midi-folder-group')`) to physically relocate elements between the sidebar and the drawer. When I restructured `[REDACTED_PERSON]`'s ids, I only checked *my own* files (`[REDACTED_PERSON]`, `[REDACTED_PERSON]`, `[REDACTED_PERSON]`, `[REDACTED_PERSON]`) for references — I never grepped the *whole* codebase for the old ids before deleting them. `[REDACTED_PERSON]` held three stale references (`#midi-folder-group`, `#midi-online-group`, plus the new `#midi-source-group` was never added anywhere). `getElementById` on a missing id doesn't throw — it just returns `null`, and every call site was already null-guarded (`if (midiFolder) ...`), so it failed *silently*. No console error, no crash, nothing my Desktop-only testing would ever surface.

That silent-null failure mode is the actual class of bug, and it's not specific to this feature. Two different tests would catch it, at different levels:

1. **Static reference-integrity check** (cheap, fast, no browser): scan every `.js` file for `getElementById('literal-id')` / `querySelector('#literal-id')` string literals, and assert each one exists as an `id="..."` somewhere in `[REDACTED_PERSON]`. This directly targets the actual failure mechanism (a stale id reference), runs in milliseconds, and would have caught this the moment I deleted the old ids — regardless of mobile vs. desktop, regardless of which mode. Misses only non-literal/dynamically-constructed ids (rare in this codebase from what I've seen).

2. **Behavioral visibility smoke test** (real browser, more expensive): for every mode, at mobile and desktop viewports, assert that every control inside that mode's own `-controls` panel is actually visible (non-zero bounding box, no `display:none` ancestor) once that mode is active. This catches the same bug from the *symptom* side, and also catches other causes entirely unrelated to stale ids (a CSS rule, a wrong display toggle) — but it's slower and more work to get right (deciding what "should be visible" means per mode/control).

I'd lean toward building (1) first — it's the more direct, general fix for exactly this failure mode and costs very little — and treating (2) as a good idea for later, not required to close this out. Want me to proceed that way, or do you want both before I touch the fix again?


