Partially — but not fully automatically, and it's worth being precise about where the line falls.

I checked: the doc's own definition of "primary element" is explicitly a *semantic* one ("a top-level interactive affordance a player can point to and name," as opposed to a repeated sub-item like one carousel piece or one chord-guide result). Repeated/templated sub-items are JS-generated at runtime, not present in `index.html`'s static markup at all — so a structural scan would never mistake them for primary elements; that part is naturally safe.

The real obstacle: primary elements aren't all scoped inside one predictable container per mode. [REDACTED_PERSON] (`[REDACTED_PERSON]`, `[REDACTED_PERSON]`, etc.) and `[REDACTED_PERSON]`/`#drawer-handle` live *outside* `#gravity-controls`/`#snake-controls` entirely — shared markup, relabeled per mode by JS. A scan of "everything inside `#<mode>-controls`" would miss those, and there's no existing structural signal that says "this button belongs to Gravity" the way an id prefix says "this belongs to Melody."

What I *can* build: tag every element the doc table currently enumerates with a shared marker (e.g. `data-primary="gravity"` or similar) directly in `index.html`, then have the test derive its per-mode list from `[data-primary]` instead of the hand-written `PRIMARY_ELEMENTS` map — and pair that with a static check (same style as `check-dom-ids.js`) that flags any *new* interactive element added to a mode's controls block that's neither tagged `data-primary` nor inside a container that starts `display:none` in the static markup (the existing signal for "deliberately conditional," like `#compose-edit-group`). That doesn't make tagging fully automatic, but it converts "silently forgot to update a list" into "the check stops you until you consciously tag it or exclude it" — the same shape of fix as today's id-checker.

Want me to build that now, or land today's `PRIMARY_ELEMENTS` addition first (confirmed red/green) and treat the `data-primary` retrofit as its own follow-up?


