### 2026-08-15T22:02:00.457Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Because it's deliberately round-robin, not full pairwise — and that's a documented tradeoff already in the test file (`tests/invariants.spec.js`, right above `STATEFUL_MODES`):

> "Paired with the NEXT mode in the list (wrapping around) rather than every possible pair — round-robin still puts every mode through both roles (the one switched away from, and the one switched to) across the full suite, at 1/5th the runtime of a full pairwise sweep."

The actual property INV-48 needs proven is: *does leaving-and-returning-to mode X ever get corrupted by whatever happened in some other mode Y in between?* A full n² sweep (every mode paired with every other mode) would prove that for all C(n,2) combinations, but the failure mode INV-48 guards against — shared mutable state, a leaked timer, a stale async callback repainting the wrong canvas — is a property of the *pair of modes involved*, not something that varies meaningfully across which specific *other* mode Y happens to be, once every mode has been exercised in both roles (switched away from, and switched into) at least once.

With 7 modes now, round-robin gives 7 tests instead of 21 (`C(7,2)`) — each mode plays "the one being tested" exactly once and "the other mode" exactly once, so every mode passes through both roles across the full suite, just not against every possible partner. The tradeoff: if some *specific* pair (say, [REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO] specifically) had a bug that only manifests with that exact combination and not with either mode's round-robin neighbor, this suite wouldn't catch it. Given the actual historical bugs it was built to catch (#15/#16 — a stale fetch repainting whatever's on screen, unrelated to which mode is showing; Blast/Gravity's shared `Board.cells` — a property of those two specific modes now permanently fixed by `createBoard()`), that residual risk was judged acceptable for a 5x runtime saving. Worth knowing as a known gap, not an oversight.


