I treated "Compose already has undo" as satisfying that part of the issue, since it does — `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`'s `undo()` exists and works for the common case (undo the note you just recorded). The issue's own text about Compose ("needs a lot of work, but it definitely makes sense there") read to me as acknowledging that gap rather than asking me to close it, so I scoped it out and just left a note in my research findings rather than silently expanding the task.

But the gap is real, and I should be upfront about exactly how narrow it is: Compose's `undo()` is just `notes.pop()` — it only correctly reverses "the last note I just recorded." It does **not** correctly reverse:
- `compose-delete` (removes selected notes by index, not necessarily the last one)
- `compose-rotate-cw`/`compose-rotate-ccw` (mutates positions in place)
- pasting a selected group (`copySelected`/`pasteGroup`, task #82) — pushes N notes, `pop()` only removes 1

So today, doing any of those and then hitting Undo either does nothing useful or removes the wrong thing. Given I just built the real `UndoStack` mechanism for exactly this kind of "multiple different mutators need their own correct inversion" problem in Sandbox/Blast/Life, extending it to Compose (four more mutators to wrap: record, delete, rotate×2, paste) is a natural, bounded follow-up — not a redesign.


