### 2026-08-17T01:21:35.419Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Fixed and committed as `[REDACTED_SECRET]`.

**Root cause**: `main.js`'s `controllerchange` listener force-reloaded the page whenever it transitioned from uncontrolled to controlled by the service worker — but that transition fires on *every* first-ever visit too (`sw.js`'s `activate` handler calls `clients.claim()`, which claims the page that's already open), not just when an update swaps out an old worker. So a new visitor's page rendered once and then immediately force-reloaded itself for no real reason. On localhost this self-heals near-instantly and is invisible, but on a real network it's exactly the kind of extra navigation that can leave the app caught mid-initialization — matching "sees emptiness, resolves itself on reload."

**Fix**: capture whether the page already had a controller *before* calling `register()`; only force-reload on `controllerchange` if it did (a genuine update), never on a first-ever visit.

Verified red→green (wrote the regression test first, confirmed it failed against the old code, then fixed it), and the full desktop suite (151/151) still passes, including the existing stale-cache test that exercises the same SW lifecycle.


