### 2026-08-17T01:41:30.455Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

I've written this up as `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`. Here's the shape of the plan:

**Pipeline:** internal note array → a new hand-rolled `notesToMusicXML()` (JS, no music21/Python needed) → Verovio (vendored WASM, renders SVG) → read each note's rendered x-position back out of the SVG by id → that x-map drives three things in lockstep: our own note-name/octave labels, a re-laid-out version of the existing token-timeline, and barline overlays spanning from the staff down through the timeline. Verovio's x-positions are the single source of truth everything else aligns to — that's what gets you "perfect sync" without three layers coincidentally agreeing.

**Key calls I made, flagged for your review:**
- **No music21.** Our note array already has everything needed (time/duration/midi); MIDI→MusicXML for a monophonic melody is a bounded quantize-and-emit algorithm, not something needing a full notation-intelligence library. Keeps this a self-contained JS module reusable later by Compose's own MusicXML export (explicitly deferred as Phase 2, not part of this pass).
- **Grand staff split at MIDI 60 (middle C)**, standard piano convention — flagged to revisit if a real song reads awkwardly.
- **Labels are our own text elements, not MusicXML `<lyric>`s** — same reasoning as the sync requirement: one coordinate source (Verovio's rendered note x-positions) drives everything, rather than trusting Verovio's own lyric layout to coincidentally match our overlay.
- **Bundled `midi/*.mid` songs get a one-time offline conversion** (our own converter, run once, checked in as `.musicxml`) — MusicXML becomes canonical going forward; existing `.mid` uploads keep working unchanged.

Sleep well — this is ready to pick up whenever you want to start building it.


