Here's the full current state of `docs/melody-notation-design.md` (149 lines):

---

# Melody/Compose: grand-staff notation via Smoosic (design, not yet implemented)

Requested live: show the song (or Random's generated sequence) on an actual grand staff (treble + bass, like piano notation), with each note's name+octave printed underneath it, and measure barlines that visually span the WHOLE stack — staff, the name/octave row, and the existing note-token timeline below it — as one connected block per measure. The staff is a new display sitting ABOVE the existing timeline, not a replacement for it: the existing timeline keeps doing the interactive job (scrub marker, drag-to-seek, tri-coloured upcoming notes, edge-scroll). The two views must stay in perfect horizontal sync — every note's staff position, its name/octave label, and its existing timeline token all line up at the same x-coordinate, with barlines connecting straight through all three.

**Revision note:** an earlier draft proposed a hand-rolled `notesToMusicXML()` plus Verovio (display-only rendering). Superseded after two pieces of pushback: (1) rhythm quantization is a genuinely hard, well-solved problem, not worth reinventing badly; (2) Compose wants an actual notation EDITOR, not just a read-only renderer. Verovio and the hand-rolled serializer are dropped.

**## The pivot: Smoosic, not Verovio + hand-rolled MusicXML**
[REDACTED_SECRET] One library instead of two; quantization only needed for the one case that still needs any (see below); coordinate sync comes from the editor's own note-position API instead of screen-scraping SVG.

**## Why not music21 (Pyodide) after all**
Checked concretely: Pyodide core ~6.4MB before any package, ~5s first load, music21 adds more on top — all to solve a problem Smoosic's own MIDI import already solves in-browser with no second language runtime. Kept as a fallback option for a one-off offline conversion script only, not planned for day one.

**## Open, unresolved question: what does this make Compose?**
Does Tonnetz-tap stay primary with Smoosic as a synced secondary editing view, or does staff-click become primary? Leaning toward the former (matches the app's identity, existing undo system) but explicitly left as your call.

**## LibreJS compliance**
MIT is free and LibreJS-recognized, but compliance isn't automatic — LibreJS scans the *served file* for a license comment; minified npm builds often strip it. Action item: verify whatever build gets vendored actually carries one. Verification path: `npm run test:librejs`.

**## MusicXML is the canonical/write format; MIDI is import-only (correction)**
This is the section you just prompted the fix on: bundled songs and Compose's Save both write/store MusicXML (notation from the start, nothing inferred); MIDI survives only as an import path for the two sources that never had notated rhythm to begin with — uploaded `.mid` files and Random's generated sequence — both funneled through Smoosic's MIDI import via the existing `writeMIDI()`.

**## Open items to settle when implementation actually starts**
Confirm Smoosic's real API (read-only mode, note-coordinate access) against its Demos repo; clef-split threshold; barline-overlay mechanics; vendoring/build mechanics; structural (not pixel-level) testing approach.

