Primary for **note entry and editing** — i.e., which interaction surface is the main way you actually build/change a composition, as opposed to just displaying it.

Concretely, Compose currently supports these actions entirely through Tonnetz-tap gestures:
- **Add a note**: tap a hex cell (records it into `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`)
- **Select notes**: tap/drag-select on the lattice
- **Delete selected**: a button, acting on the tap-based selection
- **Insert**: insert a note after the current selection
- **Translate**: drag the selection to a new position (shifts pitch/time)
- **Rotate**: rotate the selection around a pivot

The open question is whether *those same actions* — add, select, delete, insert, move, rotate — stay driven by tapping/dragging on the hex Tonnetz (with Smoosic just rendering a synced, read-along staff view above it), or whether clicking/dragging directly on the staff notation becomes the way you do them, with Tonnetz-tap demoted to secondary or dropped to display-only.

It's not an all-or-nothing question either — a middle ground exists where both surfaces can edit (tap a hex *or* click a notehead, either one updates the same underlying `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` and both views refresh), and "primary" then just means which one is documented/default/gets new features first. I left it open rather than picking, since it changes how much of Compose's existing test suite and undo-stack wiring (`[REDACTED_SECRET]`, [REDACTED_SECRET] — all currently keyed off Tonnetz-native mutators) stays as-is versus needs rethinking around a second input surface.


