Findings on real candidates:

- **MuseScore Studio** — GPLv3, genuinely free and open source, MusicXML import/export is mature and actively maintained. The free/open-source gold standard, matches this project's own licensing stance well. Downside for a "link out" use case: it's a desktop app, not web-based — no zero-install hand-off.
- **Flat.io** and **Noteflight** — web-based, no install, real MusicXML import/export on their free tiers (Flat's free plan reportedly includes the full editor + MusicXML import with no account required). Commercial/freemium though, not free software.
- **Smoosic's own hosted demo** (`[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`, from the npm metadata I already pulled while evaluating it as a dependency) — free, web-based, MIT-licensed, full editor. Rejected as something to *embed* (25MB unminified dev bundle, wrong shape as a dependency) but that's a completely different question from whether its own hosted app is a reasonable thing to *link to* — as a standalone destination it's actually a decent fit: free, no install, MIT.

Sources: [MuseScore GitHub](https://github.com/musescore/MuseScore), [Flat.io best notation software 2026](https://[REDACTED_SECRET]/), [ScoreLabs browser-based comparison](https://scorelabs.app/blog/best-free-browser-based-notation-software)

**Now the sharper question — is sending them MusicXML even the right move?** I don't think it's a clean yes, for two separate reasons:

1. **The mechanical hand-off problem is unchanged from #53's original finding for Signal, and applies to all of these too.** None of these tools' public interfaces let us inject a local blob across origins without a fetchable public URL — Tonncade's saved file is either a File System Access folder handle or a downloaded blob, never a URL. So "link out" in practice means: download the file, open MuseScore/Flat/Smoosic yourself, upload it there. A plain link with no code behind it accomplishes that exactly as well as any integration would — there's no real one-click hand-off to build regardless of which tool gets picked.

2. **The original motivation for #53 is substantially weaker now than when it was written.** It existed because Compose had *no* real editing power beyond tap-record and a naive quantize checkbox — "go use a real tool for precision work" made sense when there was no in-app alternative. This design now gives Compose real MusicXML-native staff-click editing, ties, and explicit key/time-signature settings — exactly the kind of precision the original item wanted to outsource. And even with a link, nothing round-trips cleanly: dynamics/ornaments/lyrics/polyphony someone adds externally get silently dropped again the moment the file comes back into Tonncade, since those stay explicitly excluded. The feature's ceiling is low on both ends.

My honest read: don't build a "send to X" feature at all. At most, mention in a help string "for full notation-editor power, export and open it in MuseScore or Flat.io" as documentation — since the coded version and the documented version reduce to the same user action (download, then re-upload elsewhere yourself), building UI for it doesn't buy anything real.

